QEMU 11.1.1 gdbstub, user mode: a stop holds the other threads, and no thread is made or ended under it.

paslang's debugger check runs a program under QEMU's gdb stub with a
breakpoint that is false at every stop but one
(scripts/check_debug_growth.py, scripts/inject_debug_growth.py), so gdb
removes the breakpoint, steps and inserts it again at each of the
others, while the program's runtime starts its threads. Once, in a make
check on a busy machine (paslang P582), the program ran past the stop
that was true to its end, and gdb said

  warning: error removing breakpoint -1 at 0x4142c4
  [Inferior 1 (process 53619) exited normally]

In user mode the thread that stops talks to gdb while the others run on
(gdb_handlesig() waits in the stopping thread alone), and three things
the stub does for every CPU of the list went wrong under a thread that
was being made or ended. scripts/check_gdbstub_threads.py is the case
alone, a guest whose one thread calls a routine two thousand times
while another makes threads that end at once, under gdb with a
breakpoint at the routine that is true at the last call; what it
showed, each read in the sources and seen under a host gdb:

- The breakpoint lost, in every session within a second. cpu_copy()
  (linux-user/main.c) makes a new thread's CPU with cpu_create(), whose
  realize puts it in the list of CPUs (cpu_list_add(), cpu-common.c),
  and only then gives it its creator's breakpoints; it runs on the
  creator's thread, in a system call and out of cpu_exec, which the
  stub's exclusive section (the patch before this one) does not wait
  for. gdb_breakpoint_remove() (gdbstub/user.c) asks every CPU of the
  list and stops at the first that answers -ENOENT: the new CPU,
  between the two steps. The breakpoint is then gone from every CPU
  before it, the stopped one among them, and gdb is told of an error;
  gdb (18.1, breakpoint.c) keeps the location as inserted
  (remove_breakpoint_1() returns before it clears bl->inserted; the
  warning is update_global_location_list()'s, "This is just about all
  we can do") and never inserts it again.
- QEMU dead of its own SIGSEGV, one session in fifteen: the new CPU's
  reset (arm_cpu_reset_hold(), hw_breakpoint_update_all(),
  cpu_breakpoint_remove_all()) walking its list of breakpoints inside
  cpu_create() while the stub inserted one in it.
- "double free or corruption" at the end of gdb_handle_vcont()
  (gdbstub/gdbstub.c), one session in twelve: it sizes a table by the
  CPUs there are (gdb_get_max_cpus()) and then writes an entry for
  every CPU of the list, one made since among them; and a CPU
  unrealized and freed by its thread's exit (linux-user/syscall.c,
  TARGET_NR_exit) while the stub went through the list.

Three changes. With them the guest's two thousand stops, about two
thousand threads made and ended between them, pass in sixty sessions
of sixty, and paslang's own checks under the stub pass as before
(check_debug_growth, check_syscalls). Without the first, seven sessions
of sixty end with the emulator dead (double free or corruption);
without the second, all sixty lose the breakpoint; without the third,
one of sixty ends with the emulator dead the same way: a thread in its
exit is out of cpu_exec like one in cpu_copy(), and a stop does not
wait for it.

The two sections of a thread's making and of its exit start before
clone_lock is taken, not under it. fork_start() (linux-user/main.c)
takes them in that order, start_exclusive() and then
clone_fork_start(); a first form of this patch started them under
clone_lock, and a guest that forked while another of its threads was
being made stood for ever, the thread in do_fork() with clone_lock
waiting for the section and the thread in fork_start() with the
section waiting for clone_lock: paslang's testdata/sysprocesses, which
starts sixty-four processes from as many routines, one run in three
(the stacks of the two threads read under a host gdb). As they are,
that program ends thirty times of thirty.
scripts/check_gdbstub_threads.py runs that case alone too, a guest
(scripts/qemu/forkthreads.s) that forks while another thread makes
threads, with no gdb.

- gdb_handlesig(): the stop is an exclusive section (start_exclusive(),
  cpu-common.c) from its report to gdb until gdb resumes the program,
  as gdb's all-stop means it: every other CPU out of cpu_exec, and two
  threads that stop at once answer one after the other. The first stop,
  from main() before any CPU runs, has current_cpu NULL and takes none.
- do_fork(), a thread: the making of the new CPU, cpu_copy() from
  cpu_create() to the copy of its creator's breakpoints, is an
  exclusive section started before clone_lock, so the stub sees the
  new CPU whole or not at all.
- A thread's exit (TARGET_NR_exit) takes its CPU out of the list in an
  exclusive section started before clone_lock, with a reference of its
  own until the section is over (end_exclusive() reads current_cpu);
  what frees the CPU comes after.

--- a/gdbstub/user.c
+++ b/gdbstub/user.c
@@ -199,13 +199,43 @@
     exit(code);
 }
 
+static int gdb_handlesig_stopped(CPUState *cpu, int sig, const char *reason,
+                                 void *siginfo, int siginfo_len);
+
+/*
+ * A stop is an exclusive section, from its report to gdb until gdb
+ * resumes the program: every other CPU out of cpu_exec meanwhile, and no
+ * thread made or ended, since cpu_copy() and a thread's exit are
+ * exclusive sections too. In user mode the other threads ran on while
+ * this one talked to gdb, and what the stub reads and writes for every
+ * CPU changed under it: gdb_handle_vcont() sized its table by the CPUs
+ * there were and wrote past it for one made since (double free or
+ * corruption at its end), a breakpoint was removed from a CPU that was
+ * being made or freed, and two threads that stopped at once answered gdb
+ * together. The first stop, from main() before any CPU runs, has
+ * current_cpu NULL and no other thread, and takes no section.
+ */
 int gdb_handlesig(CPUState *cpu, int sig, const char *reason, void *siginfo,
                   int siginfo_len)
 {
     if (!gdbserver_state.init || gdbserver_user_state.fd < 0) {
         return sig;
     }
+    if (current_cpu) {
+        start_exclusive();
+        /* another thread's stop may have ended the session meanwhile */
+        if (gdbserver_user_state.fd >= 0) {
+            sig = gdb_handlesig_stopped(cpu, sig, reason, siginfo, siginfo_len);
+        }
+        end_exclusive();
+        return sig;
+    }
+    return gdb_handlesig_stopped(cpu, sig, reason, siginfo, siginfo_len);
+}
 
+static int gdb_handlesig_stopped(CPUState *cpu, int sig, const char *reason,
+                                 void *siginfo, int siginfo_len)
+{
